proofs.proof_tex:
The construction of \(\widehat\tau_n\) contains neither \(\kappa\) nor an endpoint-class label.  We evaluate the oracle inequality in \(\mathrm{thm{:}profile\text{-}adaptive\text{-}estimator}\) at one deterministic common bandwidth for each endpoint class.

Fix \(\kappa\in K\), write \(\alpha=\kappa-2\), and first suppose that \(P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa\).  Put
\[
 b_n=n^{-1/(\kappa+2)}.
\]
For all sufficiently large \(n\), uniformly over \(\kappa\in K\), the dyadic grid contains \(h_b\) such that
\[
 b_n\leq h_b<2b_n.
 \tag{E1}
\]
Indeed, \(b_n<h_0\) eventually uniformly on \(K\), whereas the smallest element of \(H_n\) is at most \(h_0/n<b_n\); consecutive grid elements differ by a factor of two.  For \(x\in B\), define the proof intermediate
\[
 w_x=\frac{a_P(x,h_b)}{h_b^\kappa}.
\]
The base-mass assumption gives \(w_x\geq c_m\).  The constant \(C_0\) in the oracle theorem may be enlarged without invalidating that theorem, because \(J_P(h,u)\) is nondecreasing in \(u\).  Enlarge it, if necessary, so that \(C_0c_m\geq C_m\).  Then, for all sufficiently large \(n\),
\[
 u_x:=\min\{1,C_0a_P(x,h_b)\}\geq C_mh_b^\kappa,
 \tag{E2}
\]
where \(C_mh_b^\kappa\leq1\) is used.

If \(C_0w_xh_b^\kappa\leq1\), the isolated-envelope assumption at \(u_x=C_0w_xh_b^\kappa\) gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 J_P(h_b,u_x)
 &\leq C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+h_b^{-1}
 \left(\frac{C_0w_xh_b^\kappa}{h_b^2}\right)^{1/\alpha}\right\}\\
 &=C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+C_0^{1/\alpha}w_x^{1/\alpha}\right\}.
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{E3}
\]
If \(C_0w_xh_b^\kappa>1\), the same assumption at \(u_x=1\) gives
\[
 J_P(h_b,1)
 \leq C_{\mathrm{iso}}\left\{1+h_b^{-1-2/\alpha}\right\}.
 \tag{E4}
\]
The premise of this second case implies
\[
 w_x^{1/\alpha}>C_0^{-1/\alpha}h_b^{-\kappa/\alpha}
 =C_0^{-1/\alpha}h_b^{-1-2/\alpha}.
 \tag{E5}
\]
Thus, in both cases, compactness of \([\epsilon,\bar\kappa-2]\) yields a constant \(C\), uniform over \(\kappa\in K\), for which
\[
 1\vee J_P(h_b,u_x)\leq C\{1+w_x^{1/\alpha}\}.
 \tag{E6}
\]
Moreover,
\[
 \sup_{\alpha\in[\epsilon,\bar\kappa-2]}
 \sup_{w\geq c_m}
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(eC\{1+w^{1/\alpha}\}\right)}{w}<\infty.
 \tag{E7}
\]
Indeed, compactness bounds \(1/\alpha\), and the numerator is at most a constant times \(1+\log_+w\); the ratio \((1+\log_+w)/w\) is bounded on \([c_m,\infty)\).

Using \(a_P(x,h_b)=w_xh_b^\kappa\), equations \((\mathrm{E6})\)--\((\mathrm{E7})\) imply
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &Lh_b+\sigma\sqrt{
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(e\{1\vee J_P(h_b,u_x)\}\right)}
 {n a_P(x,h_b)}}\\
 &\qquad\leq Lh_b+\frac{C\sigma}{\sqrt{nh_b^\kappa}}
 \leq Cb_n.
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{E8}
\]
The last inequality uses \((\mathrm{E1})\) and \(nb_n^{\kappa+2}=1\).  It is uniform in \(x\), \(P\), and \(\kappa\in K\).  The oracle remainder obeys
\[
 \sup_{\kappa\in K}
 \frac{\log^2(en)/n}{n^{-1/(\kappa+2)}}\longrightarrow0,
 \tag{E9}
\]
because the ratio is at most \(\log^2(en)n^{-1+1/(4+\epsilon)}\).  Evaluating the infimum in \(\mathrm{thm{:}profile\text{-}adaptive\text{-}estimator}\) at \(h_b\), taking the supremum over \(x\), and then over \(P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa\), proves
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq Cn^{-1/(\kappa+2)}.
 \tag{E10}
\]

For the pervasive endpoint, a bound valid on all of \(\mathrm{Base}_\kappa\) suffices.  If \(F\subseteq B\) is \(h\)-separated, order its second coordinates.  Consecutive coordinates differ by at least \(h\), while the segment has length one, and hence \(|F|\leq1+1/h\).  Since \(h\leq h_0\leq1/8\), the definition of \(J_P\) therefore gives, for every \(u\geq0\),
\[
 1\vee J_P(h,u)\leq\frac2h.
 \tag{E11}
\]
Put
\[
 r_n=\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}
\]
and choose \(h_r\in H_n\) with \(r_n\leq h_r<2r_n\).  The same grid argument proves existence for all sufficiently large \(n\), uniformly over \(K\).  The base-mass assumption, equation \((\mathrm{E11})\), and \(nr_n^{\kappa+2}=\log n\) yield
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{x\in B}\left[
 Lh_r+\sigma\sqrt{
 \frac{1+\log\!\left(e\{1\vee
 J_P(h_r,\min\{1,C_0a_P(x,h_r)\})\}\right)}
 {n a_P(x,h_r)}}\right]\\
 &\qquad\leq Lh_r+C\sigma
 \sqrt{\frac{\log(e/h_r)}{nc_mh_r^\kappa}}
 \leq Cr_n.
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{E12}
\]
The constants in the final comparison are uniform because \(\kappa\in K\).  The oracle remainder is \(o(r_n)\) uniformly on \(K\).  Since \(\mathrm{Perv}_\kappa\subseteq\mathrm{Base}_\kappa\), evaluating the theorem at \(h_r\) gives
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathrm{Perv}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq C\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}.
 \tag{E13}
\]

Finally, let \(\mathcal R^{\mathrm I}_{n,\kappa}\) and \(\mathcal R^{\mathrm P}_{n,\kappa}\) denote the two unrestricted-estimator minimax risks in \(\mathrm{thm{:}exact\text{-}endpoint\text{-}minimax\text{-}lower\text{-}bounds}\).  That theorem, together with \((\mathrm{E10})\)--\((\mathrm{E13})\) and the fact that an infimum over estimators is no larger than the risk of the particular estimator \(\widehat\tau_n\), gives
\[
 c n^{-1/(\kappa+2)}
 \leq\mathcal R^{\mathrm I}_{n,\kappa}
 \leq\sup_{P\in\mathrm{Iso}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq C n^{-1/(\kappa+2)},
 \tag{E14}
\]
and
\[
 c\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}
 \leq\mathcal R^{\mathrm P}_{n,\kappa}
 \leq\sup_{P\in\mathrm{Perv}_\kappa}
 E_P\|\widehat\tau_n-\tau_P\|_\infty
 \leq C\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/(\kappa+2)}.
 \tag{E15}
\]
Thus the identical total estimator attains the minimax order at both endpoint classes without preliminary regime selection and without knowledge of \(\kappa\).  The converse benchmarks and their testing constructions are attributed to the banked parent analysis; the conclusion established here is simultaneous attainment by the present estimator.
